{"id": "3cc9fe9d-96d6-487d-ae3e-2634328067e7", "timestamp": "2026-10-17T14:25:36.809653", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "bf224463-1a01-4d2b-bbf2-5feef2338efa", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "### Proposed Approach for Experiment Design: Evaluating CAMUV for Causal Discovery with Unobserved Common Causes\n\n#### 1. Problem Formulation and Objectives\n- **Core Question**: How effectively and reliably can the CAMUV (Causal Additive Model with Unobserved Variables) algorithm identify direct causal relationships ($X_i \\to X_j$) and unobserved common causes (latent confounding, $X_i \\leftrightarrow X_j$) in observational data?\n- **Theoretical Setup**: We consider systems governed by non-linear structural equation models with additive noise and latent variables:\n  $$X_j = \\sum_{k \\in \\text{Pa}(j) \\cap \\mathcal{O}} f_{jk}(X_k) + \\sum_{l \\in \\text{Pa}(j) \\cap \\mathcal{L}} g_{jl}(U_l) + \\epsilon_j$$\n  where $\\mathcal{O}$ denotes observed variables, $\\mathcal{L}$ denotes unobserved/latent variables, functions $f_{jk}, g_{jl}$ are non-linear, and errors $\\epsilon_j$ are mutually independent and independent of $U_l$.\n- **Graph Representation**: Output causal graphs are modeled as Acyclic Directed Mixed Graphs (ADMGs) or Maximal Ancestral Graphs (MAGs), featuring directed edges ($\\to$) for direct causal influences and bidirected edges ($\\leftrightarrow$) for latent confounding.\n- **Objectives**:\n  1. Assess edge discovery accuracy (separating true causal directions from spurious correlations induced by latent common causes).\n  2. Evaluate latent confounder localization (correctly tagging bidirected edges $\\leftrightarrow$).\n  3. Determine robustness across sample size, confounding strength, graph density, and degree of non-linearity.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Baseline Comparisons\nTo rigorously position CAMUV against existing causal discovery paradigms, we include three baseline families:\n1. **Methods Assuming Causal Sufficiency (Non-linear & Linear)**:\n   - **Standard CAM** (Bühlmann et al., 2014) & **RESIT**: Assumes no unobserved confounders; reveals how standard additive models fail (spurious directed edges) when latent variables are ignored.\n   - **DirectLiNGAM**: Linear non-Gaussian model assuming causal sufficiency.\n2. **Constraint-Based Methods Handling Latent Confounders**:\n   - **FCI (Fast Causal Inference)** and **RFCI**: Standard conditional independence-based methods yielding PAGs with ancestral and confounding statements.\n3. **Functional / Non-Gaussian Methods Handling Latent Confounders**:\n   - **Latent LiNGAM / RepLiNGAM**: Linear non-Gaussian discovery allowing latent common causes; highlights CAMUV's advantage under non-linear functional mechanisms.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Synthetic & Benchmark Data Setup\n1. **Synthetic Data Generation**:\n   - **Topologies**: Erdős-Rényi (ER) and Scale-Free (SF) DAGs with $p \\in \\{5, 10, 20, 50\\}$ observed nodes.\n   - **Latent Injection**: Introduce $q \\in \\{1, 2, \\dots, \\lceil 0.3p \\rceil\\}$ latent confounders $U_l$, each connected to 2 or more observed nodes, creating confounded pairs ($X_i \\leftrightarrow X_j$) with and without direct edges ($X_i \\to X_j$).\n   - **Mechanisms**: Additive non-linear functions (e.g., cubic splines, trigonometric $\\sin(x)$, sigmoid, polynomial).\n   - **Noise Distributions**: Gaussian and non-Gaussian (e.g., Laplace, Uniform) to test sensitivity to distributional assumptions.\n   - **Systematic Sweeps**:\n     - Sample size: $N \\in \\{200, 500, 1000, 2000, 5000\\}$.\n     - Confounder strength ratio: $Var(g(U)) / (Var(g(U)) + Var(\\epsilon)) \\in \\{0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9\\}$.\n2. **Semi-Synthetic / Benchmark Datasets**:\n   - **Masked Real Network (e.g., Sachs protein signaling)**: Hide select intermediate and hub nodes to simulate genuine biological latent confounders, measuring CAMUV's ability to reconstruct the induced mixed graph.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Evaluation Metrics\n- **Directed Edge Recovery**: Precision, Recall, and F1-score for directed edges ($X \\to Y$).\n- **Latent Confounder Recovery**: Precision, Recall, and F1-score for bidirected edges ($X \\leftrightarrow Y$).\n- **Overall Structural Accuracy**:\n  - Structural Hamming Distance (SHD) adapted for mixed graphs.\n  - Structural Intervention Distance (SID) where applicable.\n  - False Positive Spurious Rate (directed edges incorrectly inferred in place of confounders).\n- **Computational Cost**: Wall-clock runtime and memory scaling as functions of $p$ and $N$.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. Analysis Plan\n- **Primary Comparative Study**: Performance ranking (CAMUV vs. Baselines) across synthetic benchmarks.\n- **Confounding Strength Sensitivity**: Plotting edge F1 and confounding F1 against confounder signal-to-noise ratio.\n- **Sample Complexity Analysis**: Rate of convergence of SHD as $N$ increases.\n- **Ablation / Misspecification Test**: Evaluating CAMUV under mild non-additive interactions (e.g., $f(X, U) = f_1(X) + f_2(U) + \\delta \\cdot X \\cdot U$) to measure breakdown points.\n- **Statistical Significance**: 50 independent Monte Carlo trials per experimental condition; reported with 95% confidence intervals and non-parametric Wilcoxon tests.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. Next Steps\nUpon approval of this plan, I will formulate the full, detailed experimental design specification (including exact data-generating parameters, formal algorithm execution protocols, evaluation formulas, and verification checks).", "evidence": "Inspected workspace containing task.json and track.md. Verified environment is a design-only task with no pre-existing code repository or required experimental execution. Standard system Python 3.12 is present without specialized causal discovery libraries installed.", "assumptions": ["CAMUV operates under an additive structural causal model where latent confounders and observed parents enter additively into structural equations.", "Underlying directed graph between observed and unobserved variables is acyclic (no causal feedback loops).", "Unobserved variables are mutually independent exogenous confounders (not intermediate observed-to-unobserved-to-observed mediator chains).", "Evaluation focuses on both directed causal links and identification of unobserved common causes (bidirected confounding edges)."], "uncertainties": ["Preferred representation format for the causal graph output (ADMG with directed/bidirected edges vs PAG equivalence classes).", "Whether the experiment should focus predominantly on moderate-scale synthetic graphs (p <= 20) or high-dimensional regimes (p >= 50).", "Specific GAM regression backend intended for CAMUV (e.g., penalized B-splines, smoothing splines, or Gaussian Process regression)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_224945__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_224945__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Propose experimental design approach for CAMUV with unobserved common causes\", \"answer\": \"### Proposed Approach for Experiment Design: Evaluating CAMUV for Causal Discovery with Unobserved Common Causes\\n\\n#### 1. Problem Formulation and Objectives\\n- **Core Question**: How effectively and reliably can the CAMUV (Causal Additive Model with Unobserved Variables) algorithm identify direct causal relationships ($X_i \\\\to X_j$) and unobserved common causes (latent confounding, $X_i \\\\leftrightarrow X_j$) in observational data?\\n- **Theoretical Setup**: We consider systems governed by non-linear structural equation models with additive noise and latent variables:\\n  $$X_j = \\\\sum_{k \\\\in \\\\text{Pa}(j) \\\\cap \\\\mathcal{O}} f_{jk}(X_k) + \\\\sum_{l \\\\in \\\\text{Pa}(j) \\\\cap \\\\mathcal{L}} g_{jl}(U_l) + \\\\epsilon_j$$\\n  where $\\\\mathcal{O}$ denotes observed variables, $\\\\mathcal{L}$ denotes unobserved/latent variables, functions $f_{jk}, g_{jl}$ are non-linear, and errors $\\\\epsilon_j$ are mutually independent and independent of $U_l$.\\n- **Graph Representation**: Output causal graphs are modeled as Acyclic Directed Mixed Graphs (ADMGs) or Maximal Ancestral Graphs (MAGs), featuring directed edges ($\\\\to$) for direct causal influences and bidirected edges ($\\\\leftrightarrow$) for latent confounding.\\n- **Objectives**:\\n  1. Assess edge discovery accuracy (separating true causal directions from spurious correlations induced by latent common causes).\\n  2. Evaluate latent confounder localization (correctly tagging bidirected edges $\\\\leftrightarrow$).\\n  3. Determine robustness across sample size, confounding strength, graph density, and degree of non-linearity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 2. Baseline Comparisons\\nTo rigorously position CAMUV against existing causal discovery paradigms, we include three baseline families:\\n1. **Methods Assuming Causal Sufficiency (Non-linear & Linear)**:\\n   - **Standard CAM** (B\\u00fchlmann et al., 2014) & **RESIT**: Assumes no unobserved confounders; reveals how standard additive models fail (spurious directed edges) when latent variables are ignored.\\n   - **DirectLiNGAM**: Linear non-Gaussian model assuming causal sufficiency.\\n2. **Constraint-Based Methods Handling Latent Confounders**:\\n   - **FCI (Fast Causal Inference)** and **RFCI**: Standard conditional independence-based methods yielding PAGs with ancestral and confounding statements.\\n3. **Functional / Non-Gaussian Methods Handling Latent Confounders**:\\n   - **Latent LiNGAM / RepLiNGAM**: Linear non-Gaussian discovery allowing latent common causes; highlights CAMUV's advantage under non-linear functional mechanisms.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 3. Synthetic & Benchmark Data Setup\\n1. **Synthetic Data Generation**:\\n   - **Topologies**: Erd\\u0151s-R\\u00e9nyi (ER) and Scale-Free (SF) DAGs with $p \\\\in \\\\{5, 10, 20, 50\\\\}$ observed nodes.\\n   - **Latent Injection**: Introduce $q \\\\in \\\\{1, 2, \\\\dots, \\\\lceil 0.3p \\\\rceil\\\\}$ latent confounders $U_l$, each connected to 2 or more observed nodes, creating confounded pairs ($X_i \\\\leftrightarrow X_j$) with and without direct edges ($X_i \\\\to X_j$).\\n   - **Mechanisms**: Additive non-linear functions (e.g., cubic splines, trigonometric $\\\\sin(x)$, sigmoid, polynomial).\\n   - **Noise Distributions**: Gaussian and non-Gaussian (e.g., Laplace, Uniform) to test sensitivity to distributional assumptions.\\n   - **Systematic Sweeps**:\\n     - Sample size: $N \\\\in \\\\{200, 500, 1000, 2000, 5000\\\\}$.\\n     - Confounder strength ratio: $Var(g(U)) / (Var(g(U)) + Var(\\\\epsilon)) \\\\in \\\\{0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9\\\\}$.\\n2. **Semi-Synthetic / Benchmark Datasets**:\\n   - **Masked Real Network (e.g., Sachs protein signaling)**: Hide select intermediate and hub nodes to simulate genuine biological latent confounders, measuring CAMUV's ability to reconstruct the induced mixed graph.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 4. Evaluation Metrics\\n- **Directed Edge Recovery**: Precision, Recall, and F1-score for directed edges ($X \\\\to Y$).\\n- **Latent Confounder Recovery**: Precision, Recall, and F1-score for bidirected edges ($X \\\\leftrightarrow Y$).\\n- **Overall Structural Accuracy**:\\n  - Structural Hamming Distance (SHD) adapted for mixed graphs.\\n  - Structural Intervention Distance (SID) where applicable.\\n  - False Positive Spurious Rate (directed edges incorrectly inferred in place of confounders).\\n- **Computational Cost**: Wall-clock runtime and memory scaling as functions of $p$ and $N$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 5. Analysis Plan\\n- **Primary Comparative Study**: Performance ranking (CAMUV vs. Baselines) across synthetic benchmarks.\\n- **Confounding Strength Sensitivity**: Plotting edge F1 and confounding F1 against confounder signal-to-noise ratio.\\n- **Sample Complexity Analysis**: Rate of convergence of SHD as $N$ increases.\\n- **Ablation / Misspecification Test**: Evaluating CAMUV under mild non-additive interactions (e.g., $f(X, U) = f_1(X) + f_2(U) + \\\\delta \\\\cdot X \\\\cdot U$) to measure breakdown points.\\n- **Statistical Significance**: 50 independent Monte Carlo trials per experimental condition; reported with 95% confidence intervals and non-parametric Wilcoxon tests.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 6. Next Steps\\nUpon approval of this plan, I will formulate the full, detailed experimental design specification (including exact data-generating parameters, formal algorithm execution protocols, evaluation formulas, and verification checks).\", \"uncertainties\": [\"Preferred representation format for the causal graph output (ADMG with directed/bidirected edges vs PAG equivalence classes).\", \"Whether the experiment should focus predominantly on moderate-scale synthetic graphs (p <= 20) or high-dimensional regimes (p >= 50).\", \"Specific GAM regression backend intended for CAMUV (e.g., penalized B-splines, smoothing splines, or Gaussian Process regression).\"], \"evidence\": \"Inspected workspace containing task.json and track.md. Verified environment is a design-only task with no pre-existing code repository or required experimental execution. Standard system Python 3.12 is present without specialized causal discovery libraries installed.\", \"assumptions\": [\"CAMUV operates under an additive structural causal model where latent confounders and observed parents enter additively into structural equations.\", \"Underlying directed graph between observed and unobserved variables is acyclic (no causal feedback loops).\", \"Unobserved variables are mutually independent exogenous confounders (not intermediate observed-to-unobserved-to-observed mediator chains).\", \"Evaluation focuses on both directed causal links and identification of unobserved common causes (bidirected confounding edges).\"], \"stage\": \"plan\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "nufTatyZHpOm998PnOOpwA8", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 14}
